==English==
{{was wotd|2024|November|22}}

===Etymology===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*deh₁-|*leǵ-}}
{{lbor|en|grc|δέον|t=that which is binding, needful, proper, or right}} + [[w:English language|English]] {{affix|en|-ology}} (a variant of {{m|en|-logy|pos={{glossary|suffix}} denoting a branch of learning or a study of a particular subject}}). {{m|grc||Δέον}} is the {{glossary|neuter}} {{glossary|present}} {{glossary|participle}} of {{m|grc|δεῖ|t=it behoves one to, it is necessary to, one must}},<ref>{{R:OED Online|pos=n|id=1582219015|date=July 2023}}{{R:Lexico}}</ref> from {{m|grc|δέω|t=to bind, tie; to fasten; to fetter}}, ultimately from {{der|en|ine-pro|*deh₁-|t=to bind}}.

{{senseno|en|ethics|uc=1}} (“''synonym of'' ethics”) was {{coinage|en|Jeremy Bentham|nat=the English|occ=philosopher|nocap=1}} (1748–1832) to refer to [[censorial]] or [[dicastic]] ethics (ethics based on judgment), while {{senseno|en|normative theory}} (“normative ethical theory that the morality of an action should be based on whether the action follows certain obligations or rules”) was {{coinage|en|Charlie Dunbar Broad|w=C. D. Broad|nat=the English|occ=philosopher|nocap=1}} (1887–1971) in his book ''Five Types of Ethical Theory'' (1930): see the quotations.

